# Thin agents on a smart shared substrate: a shared category of how an
# agentic system is allowed to be built. Agents are configured only from
# declared blueprints, hold no policy of their own, speak a typed
# contract, and reach the world only through the substrate's checks.
#
# A system maps its own architecture into it with a functor
# (`docs/05-functors.md`); the functor's report is the conformance
# analysis: an agent route to the world that skips the substrate is a
# failed invariant, not a review comment.

category ThinAgentArchitecture {
    capability Contracted { issuers: bind_contract; }

    objects: Blueprint, Agent, Contract, Substrate, World;

    about Blueprint: "declared, human-written structure that says what an agent is asked to do";
    about Agent:     "a model or tool that holds no policy or durable state and can be replaced without loss";
    about Contract:  "a typed, serializable request and answer: the agent's whole interface";
    about Substrate: "owns meaning, rules, provenance and verification; shared by every agent";
    about World:     "anything that outlives the call: recorded decisions, admitted structure, side effects";

    instantiate:   Blueprint -> Agent     "an agent is configured only from a declared blueprint";
    bind_contract: Agent -> Contract      "the agent answers in the typed contract" ensures Contracted;
    submit:        Contract -> Substrate  "the answer crosses into the substrate";
    act:           Substrate -> World     "the world changes only after the substrate's checks";

    entry Substrate: needs Contracted;
    invariant via: Agent -> World through Contract;
    invariant via: Agent -> World through Substrate;
}

# The same rule, read from code instead of from a drawing: who can reach
# whom at build time. An arrow `A -> B` means A's code can call or name
# B (a dependency), or touch the world (a durable write, a process, a
# network call). Generated categories map into it; see
# `demos/thin-agent/derive.py`.
#
# Agents reach the substrate only through the contract, and the world
# only through the substrate. An agent may call its own model endpoint
# (Remote): from source alone, a network call that is inference cannot
# be told apart from one that is a side effect, so Remote is kept apart
# from World and the line between them is a human's to watch.

category ThinAgentDependencies {
    objects: Host, Agent, Contract, Substrate, World, Remote;

    about Host:   "the composition root: builds the agents and the substrate and wires them";
    about Remote: "a model endpoint over the network";

    host_agents:        Host -> Agent          "the host constructs the agents";
    host_substrate:     Host -> Substrate      "the host drives the substrate";
    host_world:         Host -> World          "the host reads and writes files for people";
    host_remote:        Host -> Remote         "the host configures network clients";
    agent_contract:     Agent -> Contract      "an agent names only the contract";
    agent_remote:       Agent -> Remote        "an agent calls its own model";
    contract_substrate: Contract -> Substrate  "the contract is stated in the substrate's vocabulary";
    substrate_contract: Substrate -> Contract  "the substrate holds agents only as contract traits";
    substrate_world:    Substrate -> World     "the substrate writes records and telemetry";

    invariant via: Agent -> Substrate through Contract;
    invariant via: Agent -> World through Substrate;
}
